so earlier this week we had a discussion  about the narcissism of minor  differences and I explained that it's  really important on the one hand not to  ignore significant political differences  between people between candidates on  issues but at the same time we want to  make sure that we don't fall into a trap  of making relatively small differences  into a bigger deal than they need to be  at the possible risk of hurting our own  movements this was in the context of not  just the 2020 election but we talked  about that and it turns out that it was  extraordinarily timely for me to talk  about that at the beginning of the week  because this is very quickly becoming a  real issue in the 2020 primary now that  we have more serious contenders for the  2020 Democratic nomination there are  multiple overlapping disinformation  campaigns that are in full swing  specifically to divide the left some are  more coordinated than others others are  sort of more ad hoc or de-facto  disinformation campaigns and I'm going  to tell you what's going on and who is  doing it is a little bit less clear  right now although we are learning more  and more about that every single day so  the first question is who is primarily  being targeted by these disinformation  campaigns and it's primarily Kamala  Harris Elizabeth Warren Bernie Sanders  and interestingly bethe O'Rourke who's  not actually even a declared candidate  right now he's not even said that he's  running for president in 2020 those are  the primary four targets of these  disinformation campaigns the campaigns  are primarily happening on Twitter  reddit they are stemming partially from  4chan and are also visible on YouTube as  well as just in the comment sections of  news websites that are reporting on  these campaigns and possible campaigns  tactics that are being used these will  be familiar to many of you  tactic number one distort the positions  of candidates so that comparing them  becomes more difficult because you're  comparing them based on false no  as to what their positions are tactic  number one tactic number two spread  insulting memes ad homonyms and son in  some cases they're not even really at  homonyms in the sense that they are  argument critiques on the basis of  personal characteristics sometimes  they're just insults not connected to  any particular policy number three  foment hashtags that are critical and or  insulting of the candidates using a  specific cluster of accounts that has  been identified by a number of different  data trackers online in the specifics in  the particulars a lot of these campaigns  are racially oriented one example for  just to give you one data point was the  idea that in remember that New Year's  Eve roughly around New Year's Eve  Elizabeth Warren Instagram stream where  she had a beer one of the false claims  was that she had visible during that  stream a blackface doll in the  background it simply wasn't true and  this was used to try to start creating  racial tensions around Elizabeth  Warren's campaign for example now before  people email me angrily saying no no  David those weren't BOTS or coordinated  campaigns or shills that was me I'm not  a bot I genuinely thought I don't know  Elizabeth Warren had a blackface doll on  camera behind her that may be true but  that doesn't counteract the reality that  a number of these data science firms  including guardians AI has specifically  identified a cluster of accounts that  are posting and amplifying certain memes  and stories in a very coordinated effort  there are accounts that are the same  accounts identified as spreading  misinformation during the 2018 midterm  run up and participating in a lot of the  same stuff and shockingly 2 to 15% of  all Twitter mentions of some of these  candidates originated from this cluster  of only 200 accounts which is just an  incredibly small number to be  responsible for this amount of the memes  and  disinformation and then of course they  become 